[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Error Boundaries: Handling Component Errors in React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React components, like any JavaScript code, can throw errors. These errors, if unhandled, can crash the entire application and provide a poor user experience. Error boundaries are React components that catch JavaScript errors anywhere in their child component tree, log those errors, and display a fallback UI instead of the component tree that crashed. Error boundaries catch errors during rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors of the whole tree below them."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What Error Boundaries Are Not"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"It's important to understand what error boundaries *cannot* catch:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Event handlers (learn more in the next section)"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Asynchronous code (e.g., `setTimeout` or `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks)"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Server-side rendering"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Errors thrown in the error boundary itself (rather than its children)"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an Error Boundary"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"An error boundary is a class component that defines either (or both) of these lifecycle methods:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`static getDerivedStateFromError(error)`: Use this to render a fallback UI after an error has been thrown."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`componentDidCatch(error, info)`: Use this to log error information."},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's a basic example of an error boundary component:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    // Update state so the next render will show the fallback UI.
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, info) {
    // Example "componentStack":
    //   in ComponentThatThrows (created by App)
    //   in App
    console.error("Caught an error:", error, info.componentStack);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      // You can render any custom fallback UI
      return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;
    }

    return this.props.children; 
  }
}

export default ErrorBoundary;"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the Error Boundary"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now you can use this `ErrorBoundary` component to wrap any part of your application where you want to catch errors. For example:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import ErrorBoundary from './ErrorBoundary';

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary>
      <ComponentThatMightThrow />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"If `ComponentThatMightThrow` throws an error, the `ErrorBoundary` will catch it, log the error, and render the fallback UI (in this case, `<h1>Something went wrong.</h1>`)."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Granularity of Error Boundaries"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The granularity of error boundaries is up to you. You can wrap top-level route components to display a generic "Something went wrong" message to the user. You can also wrap individual widgets or components to protect the rest of the application from crashing.  The more granular, the better the user experience, as only the failing component will be replaced with the fallback UI."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Wrapping a Component List"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Imagine you have a list of components, and one of them might throw an error. You can wrap each component in an `ErrorBoundary`:"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"function ComponentList(props) {
  return (
    <div>
      {props.components.map((Component, index) => (
        <ErrorBoundary key={index}>
          <Component />
        </ErrorBoundary>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"If one of the `Component` instances throws an error, only that component will be replaced with the fallback UI. The rest of the list will continue to render normally."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Boundaries and Event Handlers"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Error boundaries do *not* catch errors inside event handlers. This is because event handlers run *after* the initial render, and error boundaries only catch errors during rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors. To handle errors in event handlers, you need to use standard JavaScript `try...catch` statements."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"class MyComponent extends React.Component {
  handleClick = () => {
    try {
      // Code that might throw an error
      this.setState({ value: this.props.data.undefinedProperty });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error in handleClick:', error);
      // Handle the error (e.g., display an error message)
      this.setState({ errorMessage: 'An error occurred!' });
    }
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <button onClick={this.handleClick}>Click Me</button>
        {this.state.errorMessage && <p>{this.state.errorMessage}</p>}
      </div>
    );
  }
}"},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use error boundaries strategically to wrap potentially problematic components."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using a top-level error boundary to catch unexpected errors and display a generic error message."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Log errors in `componentDidCatch` to help with debugging."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provide a user-friendly fallback UI that doesn't reveal sensitive information."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Remember that error boundaries are only for handling unexpected runtime errors. They are not a replacement for proper error handling and validation."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"Error boundaries are a powerful tool for building more resilient React applications. By catching and handling errors gracefully, you can improve the user experience and prevent unexpected crashes. Remember to use them strategically and combine them with other error-handling techniques for a robust application."}]
